session_dirs_errors — implementation plan
==========================================

status: IMPLEMENTED and GREEN (2026-08-07)

infrastructure plan (not a single tool): how the server reacts to a
BAD -d SESSION DIRECTORY SET. bug fix — no spec section, no changelog
entry (the spec's "server startup and readiness" already owns the
Failed state; this plan does not touch that contract at all — a `-d`
config error is now caught and reported BEFORE readiness has any state
to be in; see "the exit path" below for why the two are disjoint).

prerequisite: the Exn.message YXML decode fix (ROOTS_ANALYSIS.md
"Finding 2", mcp_server.scala:1642-1643) landed first. It still matters
for this plan's own Bad_Dir/Root_Error details (see A2/the exit path),
even though the flagship Collision message no longer needs it (built
directly from Position.Line/File.get, not from Exn.message).

the defect
----------

Reproducer: mcp/test/repro_duplicate_session.py. Two projects, each
with its own ROOT, colliding on exactly one session name:

    proj_alpha/ROOT   defines sessions  Alpha   and  Scratch
    proj_beta/ROOT    defines sessions  Beta    and  Scratch

Observed against the real server:

    CONTROL: -d proj_alpha
      isabelle://session status: ready
      list_sessions .... ok        | Alpha  Unsorted  121

    REPRO: -d proj_alpha  -d proj_beta
      isabelle://session status: failed (Duplicate session "Scratch"...)
      list_sessions .... isError

Alpha and Beta are unrelated, validly named, independently developed.
Neither is reachable. Every tool fails — INCLUDING catalog-only tools
like list_sessions that need nothing from the prover.

Mechanism: Sessions.load_structure builds one global session graph over
every -d directory and errors on the first duplicate name
(Pure/Build/sessions.scala:871, the info_graph fold). That error is not
attributed to a root and not recoverable; it propagates out of
MCP_Session.build (mcp_session.scala:314, via Build.build) into
Failed(...), and the server serves nothing for the rest of its life.

This is a CONFIGURATION error — the user wrote a `-d` set that cannot
be loaded — but it is reported as if the build broke. The README tells
users to run exactly this configuration (README:65, "-d DIR …
repeatable"), so it is reachable by following the documentation.

the class of error, not one error
----------------------------------

Duplicate session names are one member. All of these are user -d
configuration mistakes, all currently produce the same opaque Failed,
and all are verified reachable (measured 2026-08-07 via `isabelle
build -n -d ...`):

  duplicate session name across roots
    *** Duplicate session "Form_A" (line 1 of ".../formC/ROOT")
        (line 1 of ".../formA/ROOT")

  duplicate session name against an ALREADY-REGISTERED component
    *** Duplicate session "Alpha_Beta_Pruning"
        (line 1 of ".../user/ROOT")
        (line 3 of ".../afp-2025-06-04/thys/Alpha_Beta_Pruning/ROOT")

  -d directory that is not a session directory
    *** Bad session root directory: ".../does_not_exist"
    ***   (missing "ROOT" or "ROOTS")

  ROOT syntax error
    *** [line 2 of ".../bad/ROOT"] error: bad input

  (also in the class, not separately reproduced here: "Duplicate use of
  directory", "Duplicate global theory", "Bad parent session", "Cyclic
  session dependency" — same fold, same opaque path.)

The component-collision case matters because the REMEDY DIFFERS. Two
-d roots colliding: drop or rename either. A -d root colliding with a
registered AFP/distribution session: the user must rename theirs — AFP
is shared infrastructure and is not the server's to drop. A message
that does not distinguish these is not actionable.

backing mechanism
-----------------

Sessions.load_structure decomposes into two public halves
(sessions.scala:33-42):

    val roots = Sessions.load_root_files(dirs = dirs, select_dirs = ...)
    Sessions.Structure.make(options, augment_options, roots = roots, ...)

and Root_File carries the parse LAZILY (sessions.scala:1290-1294):

    sealed case class Root_File(path: Path, select: Boolean) {
      val key: JFile = path.canonical_file
      def dir: Path = path.dir
      lazy val entries: List[Entry] = Parsers.parse_root(path)
    }

So a pre-flight pass can discover roots, force `entries` per root
inside Exn.capture, and attribute every name to a specific ROOT file
and position — all WITHOUT entering the global fold that throws. That
is the whole mechanism: the information needed for an actionable
message already exists one layer below the error.

  new: MCP_Config (mcp/src/mcp_config.scala), pure scala, no prover

    sealed abstract class Issue { def message: String }
    case class Bad_Dir(dir: Path, detail: String) extends Issue
    case class Root_Error(root: Path, detail: String) extends Issue
    case class Collision(name: String, sites: List[Site]) extends Issue
    case class Site(root: Path, pos: Position.T, provenance: Provenance)

    sealed abstract class Provenance     // which -d contributed this root,
    case class  From_Dir(dir: Path)      // or the component/distribution
    case object From_Components          // baseline (not ours to drop)

    def check(session_dirs: List[Path]): List[Issue]   // empty = fine
    def render(issues: List[Issue]): String             // the message

check() procedure (as shipped — see "deviations from the plan" below
for the two corrections found during implementation):

 1. if session_dirs is empty, return Nil immediately — nothing of the
    caller's to check, and this also skips the baseline force entirely
    on the default no--d launch (see A6).
 2. partition session_dirs by Sessions.is_session_dir; every failure
    becomes Bad_Dir. Test each dir INDEPENDENTLY — Sessions.
    check_session_dir throws on the first, so a user with two typos
    would otherwise learn about one. Report all.
 3. baseline: Sessions.load_root_files(dirs = Nil) — the component
    path (distribution ROOTS, registered AFP, the mcp component).
    Force entries ONCE; index session name -> Site(From_Components). A
    baseline ROOT that itself fails to parse is a component/
    distribution problem, not the user's -d set, and is skipped rather
    than turned into an Issue.
 4. per surviving -d dir: Sessions.load_root_files(dirs = List(dir)),
    minus the baseline keys, forcing entries inside Exn.capture. A
    throw becomes Root_Error attributed to that root path (or to the
    -d directory itself, if load_root_files throws before any
    Root_File is even produced — e.g. a ROOTS catalog naming a bad
    subdirectory). Running per-dir (rather than once over all dirs) is
    what makes Provenance knowable — load_root_files does not record
    which -d a root arrived through, and ROOTS catalogs make it
    non-obvious.

    COST TRAP — do not write the naive version. Sessions.directories
    prepends Components.directories() on EVERY call, so
    load_root_files(dirs = List(d)) returns the whole baseline plus
    d's roots, every time. Measured with AFP registered (2026-08-07):
    baseline = 933 roots, one -d = 934. Discovery is cheap (file
    existence + ROOTS text), but forcing `entries` is not — so:
    force the baseline ONCE, and per -d force entries only on the
    post-subtraction remainder (subtract by Root_File.key, the
    canonical JFile — verified to isolate exactly the contributed
    root). Written naively this is N+1 parses of ~933 roots.
 5. group all Sites by session name; a name with sites in >1 distinct
    root file is a Collision, PROVIDED at least one site is From_Dir.
    Two baseline/component roots colliding only with each other (a
    broken install, not a -d mistake) must not be attributed to a -d
    the caller didn't even pass — found and fixed during review, see
    "deviations from the plan".

policy: fail fast, do not degrade
----------------------------------

DECIDED 2026-08-07 (see "open questions" for how this supersedes the
"degrade, do not fail" policy this plan originally proposed). A `-d`
configuration error EXITS the process cleanly at startup. It does not
serve, degraded or otherwise.

Rationale: a configuration error is knowable before any expensive
work (before Build.build, before start_session — check() is pure scala
over ROOT text), and it is unambiguously the USER's to fix, not the
server's to route around. It is also hit at setup time, before a
client has completed a handshake — there is no request in flight to
answer gracefully, no session an agent is mid-conversation with. A
server that comes up anyway, silently missing a project because one
`-d` collided with another, trades a loud and immediate failure for a
quiet and open-ended one: the agent (or the human who configured the
client) has to notice the gap on their own, later, with less context
than they have right now at launch. Failing fast with a message an
operator can find in their own terminal beats that.

This also sidesteps the entire "degrade, do not fail" section this
plan originally worked through — first-vs-last -d precedence, per-
directory drop granularity, the convergence re-check after dropping a
root, the two residually-fatal cases. None of that machinery is
needed: check() only diagnoses, it never decides what to keep.

the exit path
-------------

MCP_Server.run calls MCP_Config.check(session_dirs) SYNCHRONOUSLY, as
the very first thing — before the Future.fork that runs build/boot,
and before serve() is called. Nothing is served on a bad config:

    val config_issues = MCP_Config.check(session_dirs)
    if (config_issues.nonEmpty) error(MCP_Config.render(config_issues))

`error(...)` raises an ERROR exception that Command_Line.tool (the
isabelle launcher `isabelle mcp_server` runs through) catches: it
prints each line of the message prefixed with "***" to STDERR and
exits the process nonzero, with no Scala stack trace. Verified against
the real launcher (2026-08-07) — see "the message, as rendered" below
for the exact captured output, including the "***" framing and the
nonzero exit code. stdout is never touched; it is the JSON-RPC
channel, and non-JSON there would corrupt a client's parse.

Build/boot failures (a genuinely broken heap, a theory that won't
compile) are a DIFFERENT class of problem, reachable only after this
check passes, and are UNCHANGED: they still go through Future.fork
and the existing Failed readiness path (mcp_server.scala, the
`case Exn.Exn(exn) => cell.change(... Failed(plain_message(exn)))`
branch). This plan does not touch that branch at all.

the message, as rendered
-------------------------

Actionable = what, where, what to do. Captured verbatim from a real
`isabelle mcp_server -d proj_alpha -d proj_beta` run against the
two-project collision fixture (2026-08-07), exactly as it appears on
stderr with the launcher's own "***" framing:

    *** config error in -d session directories:
    ***
    ***   duplicate session name "Scratch"
    ***     declared at /home/u/proj_alpha/ROOT line 6
    ***           and at /home/u/proj_beta/ROOT line 6
    ***     session names must be unique across every -d directory.
    ***     rename one, or drop one -d.
    ***
    *** the server cannot start until this is fixed.

exit code 2, stdout empty. Multi-line ERROR messages keep their line
structure through the launcher exactly as ROOTS_ANALYSIS.md's own
"Bad session root directory" example promised — confirmed, not just
assumed.

For the component-collision case, MCP_Config.Collision.message renders
a different tail instead of "rename one, or drop one -d" — it lists
only the caller's own From_Dir sites under "declared at", then:

        collides with a session already registered by a component:
          .../afp-2025-06-04/thys/Alpha_Beta_Pruning/ROOT line 3
        components are not yours to drop -- rename your session instead.

surfacing
---------

stderr only, via the exit path above — nothing else. The
isabelle://session `config errors:` section and the list_sessions
footer this plan originally proposed are WITHDRAWN along with the
degrade policy: there is no live server for either resource to belong
to when a config error fires, since the process has already exited by
the time serve() would otherwise start reading stdin. progress.echo
was never used for this path either — MCP_Server.run raises before
`progress` does anything relevant, and the launcher's own stderr
framing is the entire surface.

NOT used: MCP logging (deprecated in the 2026-07-28 revision alongside
roots and sampling), and no new notification — there is no notification
channel until a client has connected, and a client can't connect to a
process that has already exited.

implementation steps
--------------------

 0. PREREQUISITE (landed): decode Exn.message before it reaches
    Failed. Still needed for Bad_Dir/Root_Error `detail` strings, which
    are captured from Sessions' own Exn.message and decoded the same
    way (MCP_Server.decode_message) before being embedded — even
    though the flagship Collision message never goes through Exn.message
    at all (built directly from Position.Line/File.get).

 1. MCP_Config: Issue/Site/Provenance ADT + check() + render(). Pure,
    no prover, no MCP_Session dependency. Fully unit-testable over
    temp-dir fixtures.

 2. Wire into the startup path: MCP_Server.run calls check() first,
    synchronously, before the Future.fork; on any issue, error(render(
    issues)) and the process exits via Command_Line.tool's own catch.
    No MCP_Session/MCP_Config coupling needed — check() runs on the raw
    session_dirs the CLI already parsed, before MCP_Session.build ever
    sees them.

 3. (WITHDRAWN — see "surfacing" above; no live server, nothing to
    surface into.)

 4. Widen check() to the remaining members of the class (duplicate
    directory, duplicate global theory, bad parent, cycles) as far as
    they are detectable pre-fold; whatever is not stays covered by the
    unchanged build/boot Failed path, which was already a strict
    improvement over the pre-plan opaque propagation and remains one.
    NOT done in this pass — the plan's four Issue-producing cases
    (duplicate name x2 provenances via Collision, bad dir via Bad_Dir,
    and a syntax-broken ROOT file via Root_Error — all four now VERIFIED
    by a dedicated unit test each) cover the reproducer and the
    prompt's test list; the remaining members (Bad parent session,
    Cyclic session dependency, Duplicate global theory) still fall
    through to the unchanged build/boot Failed path today, same as
    before this plan.

deviations from the plan
-------------------------

Two corrections made during implementation, neither changing the
shape of check()/render() but both changing what counts as a
Collision:

  - baseline-only collisions are now excluded (procedure step 5
    above). The plan as first written would have reported ANY name
    spanning >1 root file, including two baseline/component roots
    colliding with only each other — misattributing a broken install
    to a -d the caller may not have even passed. Fixed by requiring at
    least one From_Dir site. (This environment's baseline has no such
    collision, so it is a defensive fix, not one this run's tests
    could exercise directly; T2 and the dedicated regression test both
    stand as evidence the fix does not introduce false positives on a
    clean run.)

  - check(Nil) short-circuits before touching the baseline at all
    (procedure step 1). The default `isabelle mcp_server` launch
    passes no -d, and there is nothing of the caller's to check in
    that case — no reason to pay the baseline-force cost (T6 below) on
    every ordinary launch.

  - Bad_Dir.message renders `dir.expand.toString`, not `dir.implode` —
    matching Sessions.check_session_dir's own wording for "missing
    ROOT or ROOTS" (dir.expand.toString), so a relative -d echoes back
    expanded rather than relative, and the message reads the same as
    the one a bare `isabelle build` would already show for the same
    mistake.

assumptions and tests
---------------------

A1. VERIFIED 2026-08-07. load_root_files + forcing Root_File.entries
    never enters the fold that throws on duplicates — the pre-scan can
    see a colliding set that load_structure refuses. Measured, same
    process, same dirs:

      forcing entries on the colliding set
        .../alpha/ROOT -> Alpha, Scratch
        .../beta/ROOT  -> Beta, Scratch      (no throw)
      load_structure over those same dirs
        throws: Duplicate session "Scratch" ...

    This is the plan's foundation and it is now fact, not inference.
    T1 [scala unit]: fixture with two colliding roots; assert check()
    returns a Collision naming both root paths and lines, and does not
    throw.

A2. VERIFIED 2026-08-07. Position.T on Session_Entry carries file +
    line, so the message can cite "…/ROOT line 5":

      Scratch line=5 file=.../alpha/ROOT
      Scratch line=5 file=.../beta/ROOT

    Note Position.File.get returns String (not Option) — the message
    renderer must handle the empty-string case, not a None case.
    T2 [scala unit]: assert both are populated for a parsed
    Session_Entry.

A3. VERIFIED 2026-08-07. Provenance is knowable by subtracting the Nil
    baseline from a per-dir load, by Root_File.key:

      baseline roots (dirs = Nil): 933      (AFP registered)
      with one -d                : 934
      delta by key               : 1 -> .../alpha/ROOT

    Exactly the contributed root, correctly isolated. See the COST
    TRAP under the mechanism for what those 933 mean for the loop.
    T3 [scala unit]: a -d root colliding with a REGISTERED component
    session is classified From_Components, not From_Dir, and the
    message says "rename yours" rather than dropping the component.
    (The collision itself was reproduced by hand 2026-08-07 against
    the registered AFP: a user session named Alpha_Beta_Pruning. The
    shipped test uses "HOL" instead, deliberately — HOL is always in
    the baseline via the distribution's own ROOTS, unlike an AFP
    session name, so the test does not depend on AFP being registered
    in whatever environment runs it.)

    T3b [scala unit, added on review]: a ROOT file that EXISTS (so it
    is not a Bad_Dir) but fails to parse produces a Root_Error naming
    that ROOT path, exercising the second Exn.capture layer (forcing
    Root_File.entries) and the decode_message path Bad_Dir/Root_Error
    details travel. Not covered by T1/T2/T3, which only exercise
    Collision and Bad_Dir; this was a gap until review caught it.

A4. MOOT (2026-08-07 policy decision) — was "dropping the later -d
    yields a structure that loads". check() no longer drops anything;
    it only diagnoses, and the process exits instead of ever calling
    Structure.make over a filtered root list. No replacement test.

A5. MOOT (2026-08-07 policy decision) — was "a degraded server is
    fully functional for the surviving roots". There is no degraded
    server. Replaced by the exit-path claims below.

A5'. VERIFIED 2026-08-07. A colliding -d set exits the process cleanly
    before serve() ever runs, and a valid multi-root config still comes
    up and serves. T5 [e2e, mcp/test/repro_duplicate_session.py,
    rewritten]: CONTROL (-d proj_alpha) reaches `ready` and
    list_sessions shows Alpha, exactly as before; REPRO (-d proj_alpha
    -d proj_beta) now asserts exit code != 0, the rendered message on
    stderr naming both ROOT paths, and stdout strictly empty (no
    JSON-RPC reply was ever served — there was nothing to reply to).
    Observed directly against the built launcher (2026-08-07):

      $ isabelle mcp_server -s MCP-Tools -d proj_alpha -d proj_beta
      exit code: 2
      stdout: (empty)
      stderr: *** config error in -d session directories: ...

    matching Sessions.check_session_dir's own "Bad session root
    directory" precedent cited in the task brief — multi-line ERROR
    messages keep their "***"-per-line structure through
    Command_Line.tool, confirmed rather than assumed.

    Two robustness fixes made on review, neither changing the verdict:
    CONTROL's "list_sessions shows Alpha" check now requires a
    session-name COLUMN exactly equal to "Alpha" (has_exact_session),
    not a substring match — a bare "Alpha" in text would silently pass
    off the unrelated AFP session Alpha_Beta_Pruning in the baseline,
    which is visible in this environment's own list_sessions output.
    REPRO's timeout-and-kill branch now sets an explicit timed_out flag
    that the verdict checks, so a hang (proc.kill() leaves a
    negative/None returncode, which is nonzero) cannot be mistaken for
    the fail-fast behavior working. Also note: REPRO_TIMEOUT's default
    (180s) covers ROOT-parsing failures fine (REPRO fails in ~2s), but
    CONTROL waits on an actual session build — a cold MCP-Tools heap
    (e.g. right after scala_build invalidates it) can exceed 180s and
    read as a false CONTROL failure; the script's docstring now says so.

A6. VERIFIED 2026-08-07 — the pre-scan does not materially slow
    startup. It forces a ROOT parse of the component baseline (933
    roots with AFP registered) once per launch that passes at least one
    -d; the default no--d launch skips it entirely (deviation, see
    above). T6 [measurement]: check() over one real -d
    ($ISABELLE_MCP_HOME/Tools) measured at 1100ms end to end (via
    `isabelle scala`, so this includes JVM/class-loading overhead on
    top of the check() body — an over-estimate, not an under-estimate).
    Budget: << the ~20s catalog (spec:469) and << the ~39s full startup
    the plan's own earlier measurements record; comfortably cheap
    enough that the originally-planned fallback (skip forcing the
    baseline, let component collisions fall through uncaught) was
    never needed.

A7. MOOT (2026-08-07 policy decision) — was "fatal cases still produce
    a readable Failed" for the -s-names-a-dropped-session and
    Structure.make-still-throws-after-filtering cases. Neither case
    exists once nothing is dropped: -s naming a colliding session
    surfaces as an ordinary Collision like any other, caught by the
    same check() before Structure.make is ever called.

open questions
--------------

- FIRST -d WINS vs LAST -d WINS for -d-vs--d collisions. MOOT — nothing
  is ever dropped, so there is no precedence to choose. (Left here, not
  deleted, as the record of a question the fail-fast policy made
  irrelevant rather than one that was resolved.)

- degrade-by-default vs fail-by-default. RESOLVED 2026-08-07: fail-by-
  default. A `-d` configuration error exits the process cleanly at
  startup rather than serving degraded. Decided because the error is
  knowable before any expensive work and is unambiguously the user's to
  fix, and because it is hit at setup time — before any client has
  completed a handshake, so there is no in-flight conversation a clean
  refusal disrupts that a degraded server would have preserved. See
  "policy: fail fast, do not degrade" above for the full rationale;
  this supersedes the "degrade, do not fail" section this plan
  originally carried in its place.

- ordering against plans/readiness_catalog. RESOLVED as a non-issue:
  that plan extracts structure/deps/store/doc_catalog out of
  MCP_Session's constructor into an MCP_Catalog computed on its own
  future, entirely downstream of MCP_Server.run's Future.fork. check()
  now runs synchronously BEFORE that fork even starts, so the two plans
  no longer share any construction path to merge — readiness_catalog
  can land in either order with no interaction at all.

- this plan's relationship to plans/readiness' own open question
  ("should a Failed server exit instead of serving errors forever?",
  left as serve-errors there). RESOLVED for CONFIGURATION errors only
  by this plan's policy — plans/readiness itself is NOT changed, and
  its build/boot Failed path is explicitly out of scope here (see "the
  exit path" above). The distinction that lets both answers stand at
  once: a `-d` config error is caught before serve() starts reading
  stdin and before any client could have connected, so exiting breaks
  no in-flight request — there is no client on the other end of the
  pipe yet, only whoever launched the process, watching the same
  terminal the "***" lines land on. A build/boot failure, by contrast,
  happens strictly after serve() is already accepting connections (the
  whole point of plans/readiness' Not_Ready/Ready/Failed split), so a
  client may already be mid-conversation by the time it fires; exiting
  there really would turn a legible error into a bare broken pipe,
  which is exactly the case plans/readiness' reasoning was written for.
  Both plans are right about the case they each actually cover.
